# Local development. Nothing here is required to run the app — see SCOPE.md §9.

# Embedded Postgres location (PGlite). Defaults to ./.pglite
PGLITE_PATH=./.pglite

# Dev-only actor selection, standing in for a session until Clerk lands at step 7.
# Any seeded email works; these exercise the three roles:
#   shelley@northwindrobotics.test     admin
#   marcus@northwindrobotics.test   travel_manager
#   priya@northwindrobotics.test    member
DEV_ACTOR_EMAIL=shelley@northwindrobotics.test

# ── Flight booking ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All three are absent by default, and the app runs fine without them: with no
# key the Duffel adapter reports itself unconfigured and the spine runs on the
# `recorded` provider, which cannot buy anything under any configuration.

# A Duffel test key (test_...) enables real search. Live keys start with live_.
#
# With one set, `pnpm duffel:capture` records what the real API actually returns
# into fixtures/live/ (git-ignored), and the conformance suite in
# tests/duffel-conformance.test.ts stops skipping and starts checking our wire
# types against it. Without one, that suite skips and the whole app still runs —
# `pnpm test` must pass on a clean clone with no keys at all.
#
# The harness creates orders, so it refuses a token that does not look like a
# test key. Test-mode orders come back live_mode:false, which the pipeline
# already records as not-spend.
# DUFFEL_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# The flag. Absent or anything but exactly "true" means dry run — the whole
# pipeline runs and writes a booking row stamped live:false. SCOPE.md §6c rail 4.
# FLIGHT_BOOKING_LIVE=false

# The hard ceiling in integer cents, enforced inside the provider at the moment
# of purchase and independent of the policy engine. REQUIRED when
# FLIGHT_BOOKING_LIVE=true: the adapter refuses to buy without one, because an
# unbounded live purchaser is not a configuration worth supporting.
# FLIGHT_BOOKING_MAX_CENTS=250000

# ── Flight status ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tracking is a second integration with a second provider, and it obeys the same
# rule: no fallback. With a key we use FlightAware AeroAPI; with
# FLIGHT_STATUS_PROVIDER=recorded, and only when it says so out loud, we replay
# the recorded payloads in src/lib/integrations/flightstatus/recorded/; with
# neither, `pnpm flights --sync` checks nothing and names the variable to set,
# and the board still renders because the flights are ours — only the readings
# are a provider's.
#
# A screen quietly replaying fixtures is indistinguishable there from a carrier
# feed, and the sentence it would produce is "your colleague will make move-in".
# So the replay says so on the page.
#
# The AeroAPI adapter is written to the published v4 schema and has never been
# run against a live key. See the header of
# src/lib/integrations/flightstatus/aeroapi/client.ts.
# AEROAPI_KEY=
# FLIGHT_STATUS_PROVIDER=recorded

# ── Auth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both keys present switches `getActor()` from DEV_ACTOR_EMAIL to real Clerk
# sessions, and the switch is one-way: with Clerk configured, DEV_ACTOR_EMAIL is
# never consulted again, even if a lookup fails. Absent, the app runs on the dev
# seam with no accounts of any kind. SCOPE.md §3.
#
# Signing in is not provisioning: a verified Clerk account whose email matches no
# `users` row gets a "no access" page, and nothing is created for it.
#
# Set BOTH or NEITHER. Exactly one is now a startup error rather than a silent
# fall back to the dev seam — see src/lib/auth/mode.ts. The publishable key is
# the one a host injects for you and the secret key is the one you have to
# remember, so "publishable only" is the realistic mistake, and its old
# behaviour was to serve a seeded admin to everyone with no sign-in.
#
# With both set, `pnpm clerk:verify` prints what the Backend API really returns
# for each user next to what our code assumes about it.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
# CLERK_SECRET_KEY=

# ── The assistant (step 15) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The conversational concierge at /assistant. Same rule as every other provider
# in this app: **no fallback.** With no key and no explicit choice the screen
# says which variable would fill it and answers nothing.
#
# The temptation to fall back is worse here than anywhere else, because prose
# carries no provenance on its face — "the crate will not make the dock" reads
# identically whether a model reasoned it out of live rows or a keyword matcher
# guessed. So `scripted` has to be asked for out loud, and it is additionally
# built so that it cannot assert anything: it chooses which tools to run, the
# tools run for real against the real database as the real actor, and it closes
# with a fixed disclosure. Every figure on a scripted run is live; the only
# canned part is the sentence saying so.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ASSISTANT_PROVIDER=scripted
# ASSISTANT_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# ── The CRM (step 19) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The return side of §8. The cost side is entirely ours and needs none of this;
# with nothing set here, every show has a cost, no pipeline, and /roi says so in
# those words rather than showing $0 — because "this show produced no pipeline"
# and "nobody has looked" are opposite answers and only one is a reason to stop
# doing a show.
#
# Same rule as every other provider: **no fallback.** It matters more here than
# anywhere. Nobody spends money on a delay reading; a pipeline figure sits next
# to a cost figure on the screen a budget is set from, and a page quietly
# serving replayed opportunities is indistinguishable there from a connected
# CRM. So `recorded` has to be asked for out loud — and even then every ratio
# derived from it is withheld rather than printed with a caveat, because a
# reader who has learned to skim a banner has not learned to skim a multiple.
#
# SALESFORCE_ACCESS_TOKEN is a connected-app token. There is no refresh dance
# yet: there is nowhere in this workspace to keep a refresh token until hosting
# (step 21), so a 401 is reported as "replace this variable" rather than retried
# into a loop.
#
# SALESFORCE_ATTRIBUTION_FIELD is the API name of the custom field the write
# half of §8b targets, e.g. Trade_Show_Source__c. It is **never defaulted**: a
# custom field is created by the customer's admin, so a guessed name either
# fails on every record or — worse — succeeds against a field that means
# something else in their org and overwrites it. With it unset, reads work and
# the write is skipped with that sentence.
#
# CRM_PROVIDER=hubspot is a *recognised* choice that is declared and not built.
# It answers with what finishing it takes rather than "not a CRM this app has",
# because the second sentence is false and would send somebody to check their
# spelling. See src/lib/integrations/crm/hubspot/client.ts.
#
# SALESFORCE_INSTANCE_URL=https://your-org.my.salesforce.com
# SALESFORCE_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# SALESFORCE_ATTRIBUTION_FIELD=
# CRM_PROVIDER=recorded
